NAME
     netbench - what the network stack costs

SYNOPSIS
     netbench [-t SECONDS] [-p PORT] [-s SIZE] [-l | -c HOST]
              [stream|rr|connect|udp ...]

DESCRIPTION
     netbench measures one connection at a time and prints what it
     managed. With neither -l nor -c it does the whole thing to itself
     over the loopback address, forking a server of its own: that is the
     stack with no device underneath it. With -l it listens and with
     -c HOST it connects, which is how the interesting number is taken
     -- the same test run from a guest to its host crosses the software
     switch and the guest's virtio-net, and the difference between the
     two is what a guest pays for its network.

     The tests:

     stream   bytes a second through one connection, one way.
     rr       one byte there and back: round trips a second, and the
              latency of one.
     connect  connect, exchange a byte, close: connections a second.
     udp      datagrams a second, one way.

     With no test named it runs stream and rr.

OPTIONS
     -t SECONDS  how long each test runs (default 3).
     -p PORT     the port to listen on or connect to (default 5555).
     -s SIZE     bytes per write, and per datagram (default 4096).
     -l          listen, and serve whatever a client asks for.
     -c HOST     connect to HOST and measure against it.

EXAMPLES
     The stack with nothing under it:

           netbench -t 2 stream rr

     A guest measuring what its NIC costs, with the host running
     `netbench -l' first:

           netbench -c 10.0.2.15 -t 2 stream rr

NOTES
     A guest cannot be the server for this unless the port is published
     (see vmm(1) `port'): a frame arriving at a guest is delivered only
     if the guest asked for it, and an unsolicited connection is not
     that. Measuring with the guest as the client needs no such hole.

     The same program builds and runs on Linux, which is the point: a
     number with nothing to compare it against says very little.

SEE ALSO
     schedbench(1), fsbench(1), nc(1), vmm(1)
